{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-352",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Token Enforcement on /set-db and /set-lang State-Changing Routes",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing CSRF Token Enforcement on /set-db and /set-lang State-Changing Routes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The state-changing routes `POST /set-db` and `POST /set-lang` in the CMT Dashboard Laravel application do not enforce the Laravel CSRF token. A request that omits the `_token` field entirely is processed identically to a valid one and mutates the caller's server-side session state, whereas `POST /login` correctly rejects tokenless requests with HTTP 419. This allows a cross-site request to change the active database/tenant context and the interface language of an authenticated user."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The state-changing routes `POST /set-db` and `POST /set-lang` in the CMT Dashboard Laravel application do not enforce the Laravel CSRF token. A request that omits the `_token` field entirely is processed identically to a valid one and mutates the caller's server-side session state, whereas `POST /login` correctly rejects tokenless requests with HTTP 419. This allows a cross-site request to change the active database/tenant context and the interface language of an authenticated user.\n\nAn attacker who lures an authenticated user to a malicious page can silently change that user's active dataset/branch selection (`cmt_db`) and interface language. Because the selected database drives which dataset the dashboard reads, a forced switch can cause the victim to view or operate on a different branch's data than intended, enabling data-scoping confusion and unintended cross-branch data exposure in downstream views. Integrity impact is limited to the victim's session-scoped selection state; the attacker gains no direct read access on their own.\n\nEnsure `/set-db` and `/set-lang` are registered inside the `web` middleware group so that `App\\Http\\Middleware\\VerifyCsrfToken` applies to them, matching the protection already enforced on `/login`. Do not add these routes to the `$except` list. Additionally, restrict `db_name` to an explicit server-side allowlist of the seven known dataset codes (CME, CMT, CSM, DATA, INIT, MAL, SBY) and reject any other value, and validate `lang` against the supported set (id, en, zh), so that the session value can never carry attacker-chosen content.",
                "markdown": "The state-changing routes `POST /set-db` and `POST /set-lang` in the CMT Dashboard Laravel application do not enforce the Laravel CSRF token. A request that omits the `_token` field entirely is processed identically to a valid one and mutates the caller's server-side session state, whereas `POST /login` correctly rejects tokenless requests with HTTP 419. This allows a cross-site request to change the active database/tenant context and the interface language of an authenticated user.\n\nAn attacker who lures an authenticated user to a malicious page can silently change that user's active dataset/branch selection (`cmt_db`) and interface language. Because the selected database drives which dataset the dashboard reads, a forced switch can cause the victim to view or operate on a different branch's data than intended, enabling data-scoping confusion and unintended cross-branch data exposure in downstream views. Integrity impact is limited to the victim's session-scoped selection state; the attacker gains no direct read access on their own.\n\nEnsure `/set-db` and `/set-lang` are registered inside the `web` middleware group so that `App\\Http\\Middleware\\VerifyCsrfToken` applies to them, matching the protection already enforced on `/login`. Do not add these routes to the `$except` list. Additionally, restrict `db_name` to an explicit server-side allowlist of the seven known dataset codes (CME, CMT, CSM, DATA, INIT, MAL, SBY) and reject any other value, and validate `lang` against the supported set (id, en, zh), so that the session value can never carry attacker-chosen content."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
            },
