      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-352",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Missing CSRF Token Enforcement on /set-db and /set-lang State-Changing Routes\n\nThe state-changing routes `POST /set-db` and `POST /set-lang` in the CMT Dashboard Laravel application do not enforce the Laravel CSRF token. A request that omits the `_token` field entirely is processed identically to a valid one and mutates the caller's server-side session state, whereas `POST /login` correctly rejects tokenless requests with HTTP 419. This allows a cross-site request to change the active database/tenant context and the interface language of an authenticated user."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:8002",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "a267be0ff279ebbbeaa8fc5577e1c696f7e27d8d1ce79c91b9f19a8970ea8a91"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6f1bed5a1317cb91137aee3e0d1952966789b5d09d2555dd4d96013c74135092",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-24 06:14:33 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:8002",
              "cwe": "CWE-352",
              "impact": "An attacker who lures an authenticated user to a malicious page can silently change that user's active dataset/branch selection (`cmt_db`) and interface language. Because the selected database drives which dataset the dashboard reads, a forced switch can cause the victim to view or operate on a different branch's data than intended, enabling data-scoping confusion and unintended cross-branch data exposure in downstream views. Integrity impact is limited to the victim's session-scoped selection state; the attacker gains no direct read access on their own.",
              "technical_analysis": "The application is a server-rendered Laravel (PHP 8.2.28) app. All three state-changing forms (`/login`, `/set-db`, `/set-lang`) render a hidden `_token` field, and `POST /login` enforces it: a tokenless or garbage-token login request returns HTTP 419 with a 6665-byte body, while a valid token returns HTTP 200. This proves `VerifyCsrfToken` middleware is active and functioning on the login route.\n\nIn contrast, `POST /set-db` and `POST /set-lang` do not enforce it. Sending the body `db_name=SBY` (Content-Length: 11, no `_token` field at all) returns HTTP 303 and the server responds with `Set-Cookie: cmt_db=SBY`, changing the previously-active database from `CMT` to `SBY`. A follow-up `GET /login` confirms the new state: the dropdown item carrying `class=\"dropdown-item active\"` is now `SBY` instead of `CMT`. The same behaviour was observed for `POST /set-lang` with body `lang=en` (no token): the page thereafter renders `<html lang=\"en\">` and the Bahasa Indonesia string `Masuk` disappears; the value is whitelist-validated so it is not an injection vector.\n\nThe routes therefore appear to have been placed outside the `web` middleware group that applies `VerifyCsrfToken`, or the token check was explicitly exempted. Because both routes are reachable pre-authentication and store their value in the session, a tokenless cross-site POST succeeds whenever the browser attaches the session cookie.",
              "remediation_steps": "Ensure `/set-db` and `/set-lang` are registered inside the `web` middleware group so that `App\\Http\\Middleware\\VerifyCsrfToken` applies to them, matching the protection already enforced on `/login`. Do not add these routes to the `$except` list. Additionally, restrict `db_name` to an explicit server-side allowlist of the seven known dataset codes (CME, CMT, CSM, DATA, INIT, MAL, SBY) and reject any other value, and validate `lang` against the supported set (id, en, zh), so that the session value can never carry attacker-chosen content.",
              "counterevidence": "The session cookie `cmt-dashboard-session` is set with `SameSite=Lax`, which prevents the cookie from being sent on cross-site `POST` requests, so a plain cross-site form POST from an unrelated origin would not carry the session and the session state would not be mutated for an authenticated victim. This materially limits the straightforward exploit path. What remains demonstrated is that the server performs no CSRF-token validation on these two routes at all (tokenless, garbage-token and cross-session-token requests are all accepted with 303 and mutate state), which is a genuine defence-in-depth failure and becomes directly exploitable if the cookie is ever relaxed to `SameSite=None`, if a browser/legacy client ignores SameSite, or if the value can be delivered via a top-level GET. The exploit was executed only in a same-site context (no Origin/Referer simulation of a cross-site origin was possible in the test harness).",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the session cookie is delivered on a cross-site request (e.g. SameSite relaxed, or the state change reachable via a top-level GET navigation) would raise this from a defence-in-depth CSRF gap to a directly exploitable cross-site state-change; conversely, if `db_name` is additionally validated against a server-side allowlist before it reaches any database connection, the downstream impact would drop to language/UI-only and the severity would fall to low.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Establish a session by requesting `GET /login`; note the server-issued `cmt-dashboard-session` and `XSRF-TOKEN` cookies and the `_token` value in the login form.\n2. Confirm the initial state: `GET /login` shows the `CMT` database entry carrying `class=\"dropdown-item active\"`.\n3. Send `POST /set-db` with the body `db_name=SBY` and no `_token` field at all.\n4. Observe HTTP 303 and a `Set-Cookie: cmt_db=SBY` response header.\n5. Re-request `GET /login`: the `SBY` entry now carries `class=\"dropdown-item active\"` and `CMT` no longer does, proving the session state changed without any CSRF token.\n6. Repeat with `POST /set-lang` body `lang=en` and no token: the response is HTTP 303 and the page subsequently renders `<html lang=\"en\">`.\n7. Control: `POST /login` with no `_token` (or a garbage token) returns HTTP 419, showing the token check is enforced elsewhere.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
